@@ instances
## Instances
An instance is one input file that the record of a statement names. An instance does not show that any harness code ran on the input.$notes

| statement | file | bytes | sha256 | path | lemma |
|---|---|---:|---|---|---|
$rows
@@ instances_proved
For each row that names a lemma, the lemma proves that every premise of the statement holds on the file. Thus the premises of the statement can hold together.
@@ instances_recorded
For each row that is recorded, not yet proved, there is no lemma yet. Nothing proves that the premises of the statement hold on the file.
@@ instances_finite
For a bound or decidability statement, the lemma proves that the instance is a valid finite input with a valid entry on both builds. It does not show that the two builds disagree on it. A disagreement of the two builds is the event that a bound statement measures, not a premise.
@@ instance_row
| `$name` | `proofs/provenance/evidence/instances/$file` | $bytes | `$sha` | $path | $lemma |
@@ instance_lemma
`$lemma`
@@ instance_no_lemma
recorded, not yet proved
@@ instance_unchecked
not proved: `oft check` does not check the lemma of a $status statement
@@ instance_path_refused-by-driver
`refused-by-driver`: the driver exits with status 2 before it calls the harness
@@ instance_path_reaches-harness
`reaches-harness`: the driver calls the harness on the file

@@ check_row
- $what ([$lines]($file#$anchor)): $stops; $live.
@@ checks_bind
A failed check marked as ending the process contradicts every halts statement below, so a proof of one implies $that on every input it covers.
@@ checks_bind_some
A failure of $subject ends the process with a status that the halts statements below that name $that exclude, so a proof of one of those statements implies $that on every input it covers.
@@ checks_bind_others
No statement here proves the other $others.
@@ checks_none
The $who makes no check of its own beyond not crashing. $claim
@@ rejects
The $who turns some inputs away by returning a value other than 0 or -1, which the driver reports as status $status, so its halts statements allow $status as well:
$rows
@@ reject_row
- $what ([$lines]($file#$anchor)).
@@ rejects_return_only
The status does not say which line returned, so a failure of a check above that reports it by returning ($lines) also exits with status $status, and these statements allow it.

@@ model_gap
**Blocked by the model.** On ordinary inputs each of these binaries $features, which this release's model treats as an error, so every statement here is false in this model for that reason alone. The program is not at fault; the statements become meaningful when the model covers it.
@@ model_gap_some
**Some statements are blocked by the model.** On the inputs they cover, the binary $features, which this release's model does not decode, so the statements marked "blocked by the model" are false in this model for that reason alone. The program is not at fault.

